AUDIO: 'ports out there. This is simply absurd. So, to explain why this docking station is so special and expensive, we need to take a step back and look at bandwidth . When I got my first MacBook Pro and started'

[01:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'looking at USB hubs and docking stations, I couldn\'t understand why one cost $20 and another, like this one, cost $650. As you may know, there is a huge difference between USB cables.'

[01:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Some of them can transmit a lot of energy, some can\'t, some are incredibly fast, and most are incredibly slow. When you connect devices via a cheap USB cable or a cheap USB hub or docking'

[02:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'station, in many cases they will work, and sometimes you won\'t even notice any problems. For example, if you connect a keyboard, mouse, or printer, it doesn\'t matter if'

[02:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'they operate at USB 2.0 speeds—they will work the same. The problem arises when you start connecting much more complex and expensive peripherals. For example, external SSD drives, network cables, or, most importantly,'

[02:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'computer monitors. You may have a 4K monitor, but your HDMI cable, hub, or docking station may limit the resolution to 1080p. Or your monitor may support a 120Hz refresh rate, but'

[02:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'be limited to 60 or even 30 frames per second. The problem is that macOS will not warn you about this, and in some cases it is impossible to find out these details in the system at all. You won\'t even know'

[02:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'how your devices work and you\'ll get terrible results. You will blame your laptop, when in fact the problem was with the cable or hub all along. So the very first thing you need to find out'

[03:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'before looking for your first docking station is the speed of the ports on your MacBook. You would think I would just explain this now in a video, but it\'s incredibly complicated'

[03:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'since Apple changes port speeds every year depending on the laptop model or screen size. Some laptops have ports of different speeds on the same chassis, so you have to remember'

[03:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'where exactly you plug the device . Additionally, over the past 2 years with M4 and M5-based laptops, they have actually doubled the port speeds: from 40 gigabits per second to 80 gigabits per second. The conclusion is'

[03:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this: you will have to do your own research, just ask the AI, enter your laptop model, and it will tell you exactly what speed your ports have. Most Mac users have USB-C ports, which can transfer data at'

[03:55] [scene:talk]
